    one thing to check, and [38;5;153mnull[39m always means nobody is logged in.
  - [1m[38;5;153msetUserId()[39m rejects junk.[0m A non-string or empty id is refused with a warning rather than stored — this is what
    prevents the classic [38;5;153m"undefined"[39m-as-a-string bug, where a later form reads a truthy ID for a user who never logged
    in.
  - [1mFailed login clears.[0m As in section 2, so a failure can't leave the previous user's ID readable.

  The existing [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m/[38;5;153mconsole.error[39m calls in the submit handler stay as they are — replacing them isn't part of
  this change.

  [1mSection 4 — Testing and tooling[0m

  This is the part I can't decide for you, because the project currently has [1mno tooling at all[0m: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m declares no
  dependencies, no devDependencies, and no scripts, and there are no test files. So there's no established pattern to
  follow — anything here is a new dependency in a project that presently has zero, which is a genuine tradeoff and the
  reason I'm asking rather than assuming.

  What the design gives you if you do want tests: [38;5;153msession.js[39m is the interesting unit, and its behavior is fully
  specified by the rules in section 3 — stores and returns an id, returns [38;5;153mnull[39m when empty, refuses junk, clears, and
  falls back cleanly when storage throws. That last one is the case most worth having a test for, because it's the one
  you'll never hit by clicking around. Verifying login's write is then one assertion: call [38;5;153mlogin()[39m, read
  [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()[39m.

  My suggestion is unit tests plus lint/format, and to skip end-to-end. Vitest with jsdom covers the storage and DOM
  behavior honestly; Playwright would be real infrastructure for a two-field form. Mutation testing isn't worth it at
  this size.

  Whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints so the implementation plan inherits it.

